{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 179,
    "dDurationMs": 2081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cats are curious and fluffty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2260,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re also made of cells that are made\nof molecules that are made of atoms that are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6330,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made of particles that are made of quantum\nfields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8130,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But quantum fields are neither curious nor\nfluffy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10990,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have no subjective qualities and have\nquestionable physicality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14820,
    "dDurationMs": 3719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They seem to be completely describable by\nonly numbers, and their behavior precisely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18539,
    "dDurationMs": 1731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defined by equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20270,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a sense, the quantum world is made of math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22590,
    "dDurationMs": 3599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does that mean cats are made of math too?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26189,
    "dDurationMs": 3561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you believe the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis\nthen yes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29750,
    "dDurationMs": 1832,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so are you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36778,
    "dDurationMs": 5162,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In his essay “The Unreasonable Effectiveness\nof Mathematics”, the physicist Eugine Wigner"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41940,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "said that “the enormous usefulness of mathematics\nin the natural sciences is something bordering"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46770,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the mysterious”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49110,
    "dDurationMs": 4499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This statement was inspired by the observation\nthat so many aspects of the physical world"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53609,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem to be describable and predictable by\nmathematical equations to incredible precision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59719,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea that the universe is in some sense\nmathematical is thousands of years old."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64690,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pythagoras taught that all things are made\nof numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68030,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plato had some notions too, which I’ll come\nback to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71350,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it was the program of reductionism of\nmodern physics that really inspired Wigner’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76720,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77720,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One by one, the seemingly disparate theories\nin physics revealed themselves to be special"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83360,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cases of more elegant, underlying mathematical\nlaws until we were left with only two equations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89300,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describing all of nature: the Lagrangian of\nthe standard model of particle physics and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94430,
    "dDurationMs": 3689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein's field equations of general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98119,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All the incredible complexity we see in the\nuniverse — from biology to human culture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103090,
    "dDurationMs": 5629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "— appears to ultimately emerge from matter,\nspace and time as numerical quantities obeying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108719,
    "dDurationMs": 1891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110610,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we can describe baseline reality in\npurely mathematical terms, can we take this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116259,
    "dDurationMs": 1121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one step further?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117380,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if we say that the universe isn’t just\ndescribed by mathematics, but in a more fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123189,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way it actually is mathematics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126719,
    "dDurationMs": 4111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the proposal of MIT cosmologist Max\nTegmark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130830,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He calls it the mathematical universe hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133990,
    "dDurationMs": 6249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It states that: our external physical reality\nis a mathematical structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140239,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark makes the case for his hypothesis\nby first defining the concept of baggage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147239,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any theory in physics always comes with two\ncomponents: a set of equations, and a bunch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152140,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sentences explaining exactly how these\nequations relate to our human intuitions and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157670,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we can observe via experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160650,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, quantum mechanics is usually\npresented in textbooks as a handful of equations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165940,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along with a list of postulates relating to\nthe equations, written in plain english - baggage,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170430,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as Tegmark calls it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172620,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I have to say that his choice of the word\n“baggage” may be a hint of Tegmark’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177840,
    "dDurationMs": 4289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bias towards the primacy of math over anything\nnon-math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182129,
    "dDurationMs": 6461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark points out a pattern in the math-to-baggage\nratio in theories of nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188590,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, let’s arrange the world into a hierarchy\nof emergence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193030,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, you might say that human culture\nemerges from individual minds, which emerge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199230,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the neural dynamics of brains, which\nemerge from the actions of cells, which emerge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203540,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from molecules, which emerge from atoms and\nquantum fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208560,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In terms of scientific fields, that list corresponds\nto sociology then psychology then neuroscience"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214590,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then biology then chemistry then physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216580,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And maybe then you have a presumptive theory\nof everything at the very bottom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220940,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle, each theory in one of these\nfields could be derived from theories below"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226459,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227459,
    "dDurationMs": 5821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark points out that theories at the top\nare mostly regular human language, while theories"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233280,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the bottom are mostly math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235170,
    "dDurationMs": 6069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A sociology textbook will be filled with paragraphs\nof text, while a string theory text book is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241239,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wall-to-wall group theory and algebraic topology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243599,
    "dDurationMs": 5491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’re willing to extrapolate this trend,\nthen maybe at the very bottom of the hierarchy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249090,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our coveted theory of everything, there need\nbe no baggage at all, simply a set of elegant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254860,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256040,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we define baggage as the stuff that encodes\nthe relation between the equations and our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260979,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "human perspective, then if you remove humans\nyou remove the baggage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265750,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now we invoke the external reality hypothesis:\nthe assumption that there’s an external"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272020,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical reality that exists independently\nof us; and that humans and our baggage-heavy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276900,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minds are emergent from this baggage-free\nbaseline reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280919,
    "dDurationMs": 5471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Tegmark’s view, that leaves only math\nfor our description of that reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286390,
    "dDurationMs": 6499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there’s nothing else to, say, an electron\nbut a set of numbers describing its position,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292889,
    "dDurationMs": 7231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum and charge, can we say that the electron\nsimply is that set of numbers?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300120,
    "dDurationMs": 5169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mathematical universe hypothesis hinges\non the notion that mathematical existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305289,
    "dDurationMs": 1811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equals physical existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307100,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the most controversial part of the\nidea, but the most disturbing part is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312900,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "implication of this statement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315020,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If mathematical existence implies physical\nexistence, then, according to Tegmark, anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320060,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that exists mathematically also exists physically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323650,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More precisely, any self-consistent mathematical\nstructure that can be written down can be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329090,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "said to mathematically exist, and so it therefore\nalso physically exists in the same way that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335380,
    "dDurationMs": 1319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336699,
    "dDurationMs": 5871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, in this context a mathematical\nstructure is a set of mathematical objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342570,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that obey certain axioms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344850,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And mathematical objects are number-like entities\nsuch as the integers, the real numbers, vectors,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350569,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. and the things that relate these entities\nlike operators, functions, and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356480,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every independent, self-consistent such structure\nmanifests as a universe out there somewhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362750,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what started off as a sort of intuitive\nclaim about physics has resulted in a claim"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368060,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about an infinity of parallel universes—what\nTegmark calls the Level 4 multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374830,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a much bigger multiverse than the other\nkinds—from the level 1 multiverse you get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381380,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the infinite space generated by eternal\ninflation, to the level 2 multiverse where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387310,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fundamental constants change over that\nspace, to level 3 where the universe splits"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392830,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into parallel tracks of the quantum multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396490,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Level 4 potentially encompasses all of these,\npotentially enabling a realization of each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401580,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the lower levels for every consistent set\nof mathematical laws."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405949,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s reign this in a bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409180,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before I go believing in a new type of multiverse,\nI have just one or two questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415090,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does it even mean for the universe to\nbe “made of math”, really?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419849,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What types of mathy universes exist out there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423620,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can this idea tell us anything about why do\nwe live in a universe with our particular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428560,
    "dDurationMs": 1449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set of equations?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430009,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And is this whole idea even remotely plausible\nand testable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434660,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up - what does “made of math” mean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437410,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How can an equation or a number have a type\nof fundamental existence?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442280,
    "dDurationMs": 6449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is akin to Plato’s Theory of Forms\n- the idea that there’s a realm of idealized,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448729,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "archetypal versions of the things we find\nin the real world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452289,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a sort of concept-space, where exist\nthe quintessential notions of things - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458419,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental cat, the essence of beauty, the\nperfect sphere, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463940,
    "dDurationMs": 6319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The forms in the material world are just crude\napproximations of these Platonic ideals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470259,
    "dDurationMs": 5651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In mathematical platonism, mathematical entities\n- numbers, geometric forms, functions, operators,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475910,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. exist independently of the material world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478870,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Tegmark takes this one step further and\nsuggests that the abstract existence of math"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484690,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is all there is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486750,
    "dDurationMs": 7259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is an ongoing and unsettled  debate over whether math has a fundamental existence “out there” and is so discovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494009,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by humans, or rather is it invented by us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498479,
    "dDurationMs": 2431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now let’s just assume the former."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500910,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then we still have to ask how a bunch of abstract math can end up looking like a universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507090,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To generate a universe from equations, those\nequations have to be implemented."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511750,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Numbers need to be plugged into the cosmic\ncalculator or whatever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516180,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That suggests you need a computational substrate\nto implement the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519979,
    "dDurationMs": 3201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in Tegmark’s story, that’s not the\ncase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523180,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He defines his Platonic mathematical structures\nto include not just the raw mathematical objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528750,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like numbers and operators, but also every\npossible instantiation of them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534380,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actual implementation isn’t required - the\npossibility of implementation alone is enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539870,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to grant a mathematical structure its full\nphysical realization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544630,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this view, our own universe is just one\nof many possible mathematical structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549650,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know its full nature, but it incorporates\nthe known mathematical structures of general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554400,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity and quantum field theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557240,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can imagine an enormous - perhaps infinite\nnumber of different mathematical structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562140,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could change the number of dimensions,\nthe topology of the spacetime, the degrees"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565610,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of freedom of the quantum fields, in general\n- we could change the laws of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570060,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But completely different mathematical structures\nare possible too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572930,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the set of integers with the\nrules of arithmetic is a mathematical structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579020,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis,\nall of these have a physical existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584190,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585470,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does this mean I could whip out my portal\ngun and visit any universe that I can imagine?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590019,
    "dDurationMs": 1971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591990,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saying that all mathematical structures physically\nexist is not the same as saying that all imaginable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597130,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598690,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark’s condition is that only self-consistent\nmathematical structures exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603870,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within a mathematical structure it’s possible\nto make theorems - these are statements in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608890,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the language of that structure that are provably\ntrue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612510,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to the mathematician David Hilbert,\na consistent mathematical structure is one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617070,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which it’s not possible to simultaneously\nprove and disprove a given theorem in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622770,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623770,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark proposes that all mathematical structures\nthat do not have internal contradictions have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629560,
    "dDurationMs": 2594,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a corresponding physical manifestation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632154,
    "dDurationMs": 5466,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to explain why we see the particular\nuniverse that we do, we need to invoke the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637620,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anthropic principle in its weak version."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640690,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re naturally only going to see a mathematical\nstructure in which observers can emerge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646720,
    "dDurationMs": 7730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our structure has the internal complexity\nand fine tuning of its details so that self-aware"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654450,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical structures arise within the larger\nstructure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657700,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s us, by the way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659500,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We observe the structure from the inside, when you play time in the right direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664200,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s also an outside view, where\nthe whole structure, including all points"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668470,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time, just exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670690,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is related to the idea of block time\nin relativity, in which the past and future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675430,
    "dDurationMs": 3899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an eternal existence when viewed from\noutside reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679329,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that all sounds totally reasonable and\nintuitive, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684139,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685139,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at some possible objections."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688850,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several of the great thinkers of modern physics\nfelt very strongly that mathematics is NOT"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693399,
    "dDurationMs": 2911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fundamental aspect of external reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696310,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather, they believed that our mathematical\ndescriptions of nature were at best models"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701120,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our experience of reality. These sorts of sentiments have been expressed by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706450,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Arthur Eddington, James Jean, Erwin Schrodinger, Neils Bohr, and Werner"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710839,
    "dDurationMs": 1661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg, among others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712500,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jean described the mathematical methods of\nphysics as being unable to put us in contact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717930,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with ultimate reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720080,
    "dDurationMs": 5689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead they lead to a shadow-world of symbols,\nas Eddington put it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725769,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s actually some good evidence\nthat math is really just a limited human construction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730470,
    "dDurationMs": 3109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than a perfect, cosmic language."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733579,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a long time it was thought that all statements\nin a good mathematical structure had to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739139,
    "dDurationMs": 6801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unambiguously either true or false, and that\nany truth value could be rigorously proven."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745940,
    "dDurationMs": 6089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the 1930s, the mathematician Kurt Gödel\nrocked the foundations of math with his famous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752029,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incompleteness theorems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753699,
    "dDurationMs": 6181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s way too much to unpack this in detail,\nbut for our purposes, it says that in any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759880,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical framework that contains at least\nbasic arithmetic, it’s possible to write"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764639,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down mathematical statements that are neither\ntrue nor false - they cannot be proved right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770340,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or wrong using the rules of that mathematical\nframework."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774140,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say such statements are undecidable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778360,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the mathematical structure that describes\nour world contains equations that are neither"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784100,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true nor false, then there are laws of physics\nthat are neither true nor false."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788500,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This violates one the the basic tenets of\nthe mathematical universe hypothesis - that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793890,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its mathematical structures must be internally\nconsistent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797560,
    "dDurationMs": 6149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also gives us some doubt that a Platonic\nworld of perfect math is even a meaningful"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803709,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concept."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804709,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The incompleteness theorems seem problematic,\nbut perhaps we can sidestep the issue by saying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809830,
    "dDurationMs": 5699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the only mathematical structures that\nexist are the ones that are fully decidable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815529,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way to do that is to demand that the structure\nonly contain computable functions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821580,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A computation is the abstract concept of a\nprogram that takes in some numerical value,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827800,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does some operations on it using a computational\nmodel like a Turing machine, and spits out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832880,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some other numerical value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834940,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a function can be computed then presumably\nit’s decidable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840750,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However not all computations are immune to\nundecidability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844660,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The halting problem of Alonzo Church and Alan\nTuring states that it’s not even possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849620,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to determine whether any given function will\nbe computable in a finite number steps, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854740,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is related to Godel undecideability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858060,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To satisfy the self-consistency requirement\nof the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis, we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862560,
    "dDurationMs": 6329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now need to require that, to qualify for physical\nexistence, a mathematical structure only includes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868889,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "functions that can be executed as computations\nthat require finite steps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875279,
    "dDurationMs": 6721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With all this in mind, Tegmark posits an updated\nMathematical Universe Hypothesis called the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882000,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Computational Universe Hypothesis. It imbues\nphysical existence to all computable mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890620,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But note that he’s still not requiring that\nthe computations actually be run—rather,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895480,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they exist by virtue of being in-principle\ncomputable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899190,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark is certainly not the first to imagine\nthe universe as a computation by the way—although"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905220,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his hypothesis would encompass all possible\nsuch universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910400,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fun side note: this idea has a weird consequence\nfor the simulation hypothesis: the idea that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916209,
    "dDurationMs": 3021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we ourselves might be living in a simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919230,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all simulatable universes exist already,\ndo we need to posit a simulator for our own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925480,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926480,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so is the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis\nplausible?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930580,
    "dDurationMs": 5699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the fact that Tegmark has to perform\nsome serious intellectual contortions to get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936279,
    "dDurationMs": 6781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around Gödel undecidability is a point against\nthe simple elegance of the basic hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943060,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The notion of Platonic existence of mathematics\nis also fraught, as I’ve mentioned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949400,
    "dDurationMs": 5641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, Platonic existence may\nnot be any more weird than the idea of physical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955041,
    "dDurationMs": 3918,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality popping into existence without prior\ncause."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958959,
    "dDurationMs": 5231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mystery of the primal cause is often expressed\nwith the question “Why is there something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964190,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than nothing?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965870,
    "dDurationMs": 6339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That framing has an implied assumption that\n“nothing” is the default state of reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972209,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you flip that, you might instead say that\nexistence is the default state, and that everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977320,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is possible exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979930,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Principle of Fecundity, or Principle\nof Plenitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984389,
    "dDurationMs": 6421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tegmark’s hypothesis refines what “possible”\nmeans in those contexts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990810,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally, is this idea even testable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995300,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not currently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996670,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only obvious test is via anthropic reasoning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000000,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should expect to find ourselves in the\nmost typical mathematical structure capable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003839,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of supporting our existence, but until we\nknow what structures are possible this is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010019,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible to evaluate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011819,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, are we made out of math?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015529,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well to find out the answer, the mathematical universe\nhypothesis is going to need to be developed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020730,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the point that it makes testable predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024900,
    "dDurationMs": 7090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps then it’ll be able to tell us\nwhy, among all of the possible level 4 multiverses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031990,
    "dDurationMs": 5827,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we find ourselves in this particular mathematical\nspacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
